## Why This Is Asked
This question tests your ability to implement basic policy enforcement in Terraform using native provider features and CI/CD gates.

## Key Concepts
- AWS provider default_tags for universal tag propagation across all resources
- Wrapper module to enforce consistent environment context
- Per-environment backend configuration in S3
- GitHub Actions gate using terraform plan output to detect policy violations

## Code Example
```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = var.region
  default_tags {
    tags = {
      Environment = var.environment
      CostCenter  = var.cost_center
      Owner       = var.owner
    }
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This checks real-world ability to design multi-cloud patterns with clean module boundaries and governance.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform modules, provider aliases, multi-cloud strategy
- Environment backends and per-environment isolation
- CI gates, version constraints, governance for cloud support

## Code Example
```terraform
provider "aws" {
  alias   = "aws"
  version = "~> 4.0"
  region  = var.aws_region
}
provider "google" {
  alias   = "google"
  version = "~> 4.0"
  project = var.gcp_project
}
provider "azurerm" {
  alias    = "azure"
  version  = "~> 3.0"
  features {}
}
module "monitors" {
  source       = "./modules/monitors"
  cloud_config = {
    aws   = { region = var.aws_region, enabled = true }
    gcp   = { region = var.gcp_region, enabled = true }
    azure = { region = var.azure_region, enabled = true }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-cloud drift in this pattern?
- How would you extend to add a new cloud while keeping tight approvals?
